  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` didn't accept prices with thousands separators, such as `\"2,450.50\"`. The regex returned `null` for them, and the code then read `m[1]` on that `null`. That threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, which `server.js` turned into a 500.\n\nEvery `ERROR POST /checkout 500` I checked in `logs/app.log` had a comma-formatted price in the payload just before it (`1,299.00`, `2,450.50`, `1,005.99`). I scanned the DEBUG payloads for non-numeric prices, and all 37 had commas. I read only the first 8 of the 50 errors in full.\n\n**Fix (`src/money.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped thousands (`1,299.00`, `1,000,000`) and strips the commas.\n- Anything else that isn't an amount (`abc`, `12,34`, `-5`) now throws `Error('Invalid amount: …')`, as the function's comment already promised, instead of a `TypeError`.\n- `server.js` still returns 500 for those bad amounts, because it catches every error. They now at least produce a readable log line. I left that alone since you didn't ask for it, but bad input arguably deserves a 400.\n\n**Tests (`test/money.test.js`):**\n- `parseAmount` handles comma-grouped prices.\n- Invalid input throws the descriptive error.\n- A `checkout` case with a `\"2,450.50\"` price, which fails on the old code.\n\n`npm test` passes: 7 of 7. I didn't run the old code against the new tests.",
